.TH CONFIGSYS.HU 5 "" "configsys" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
configsys.hu \- the configsys configuration and routing format
.PP
configsys reads Humon (\fB.hu\fR) files. This is the reference for their structure: how your per-machine config selects what to install, how \fBroutes.hu\fR describes where each component comes from on any OS, and how the two combine. It is the source for the \fBconfigsys.hu(5)\fR man page and is linked from the README.
.SH LAYERS
.PP
Every \fB.hu\fR file is a \fBlayer\fR. Layers overlay section-by-section, lowest precedence first, and the highest-precedence definition of a thing wins:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
repo (routes.hu + config.hu)  <  plugins  <  primary  <  your config
.fi
.RE
.PP
Your machine's own config (\fB~/.config/configsys/configsys.hu\fR) always wins. The \fBprimary\fR layer is one plugin you designate as trusted to carry machine settings (see docs/plugins.md) — it sits above the ordinary plugins but below your config. A file may \fBinclude:\fR other files (paths relative to the including file's directory), which sit just below it. Includes are definitions-only: their \fBcomponents:\fR and \fBprofiles:\fR merge in, but machine settings (\fBpicks:\fR, \fBmachine:\fR, \fBpins:\fR, \fBscope:\fR, … — see the settings table) and code-adjacent sections (\fBos:\fR, \fBdrivers:\fR) are ignored. The cosmetic/UI sections \fBtheme:\fR and \fBkeys:\fR are the exception: they are merged from every layer (see docs/theming.md).
.SH YOUR CONFIG FILE
.PP
Lives under \fB$XDG_CONFIG_HOME\fR (defaults to \fB~/.config/configsys/configsys.hu\fR); a legacy \fB~/configsys.hu\fR is migrated automatically on first run. Every section is optional:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
{
    machine: laptop                  // which picks: column is THIS box (default: this-machine)

    picks: {                         // the install set: one component list per machine
        laptop:  [ btop, neovim, gcc-15, gdb ]
        desktop: [ btop, neovim, steam ]
    }

    scope: system                    // default install scope for scope-honoring drivers

    include: [ ~/src/myproject/configsys.hu ]   // pull in more component definitions

    plugins: [ { source: "github:someone/configsys-opensuse"  ref: v1.2.0 } ]

    pins: { steam: flatpak }         // force a driver (binding-pin) or a provider

    driver-preference: [ native, flatpak, appImage ]   // tiebreak among valid methods

    components: { apod: {} }         // amend a route, add one, or remove one with {}

    disabled-drivers: [ glue ]       // turn an install method off here (manage your own shell config)

    adopt-installed: true            // prefer an already-installed method over the default (default on)

    reboot-advice: true              // post-op "reboot advised" via the distro's native check (default on)

    splash: ocean                    // startup wait-screen animation (name / random / off / unset=default)

    effects: reduced                 // TUI motion: full / reduced / none (unset: reduced over SSH)

    dirs: { sdk: "~/toolchains"  system: /srv/opt }   // relocate install-layout dirs

    locations: { blender: ~/dev/blender-git  kicad: /opt/kicad }   // per-component: find/manage HERE

    detect-coexisting: false         // skip the "also present (unmanaged)" pass (default on)

    keys: { components: { method: p } }   // rebind TUI keys (see `configsys keys`)
}
.fi
.RE
.SS Machine settings
.PP
Each setting has a \fBkind\fR and a \fBnature\fR (where a fresh edit lands — see the next section). \fBconfigsys config show\fR prints the same table with live values and sources.
.PP
| setting | kind | nature | default | what it does | | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | | \fBmachine\fR | scalar | machine | \fBthis-machine\fR | This box's machine name — which \fBpicks:\fR column is "this machine". | | \fBpicks\fR | map | uniform | \fB{}\fR | \fB{ <machine>: [ components ] }\fR — the install set per machine (the matrix). | | \fBuninstall\fR | list | machine | \fB[]\fR | Components staged for removal (the TUI \fBx\fR queue; runs at execute). | | \fBdispositions\fR | map | machine | \fB{}\fR | \fB{ component: seen \e| interesting }\fR triage marks; unlisted = NEW. | | \fBscope\fR | scalar | machine | \fBuser\fR | Default install scope for scope-honoring drivers: \fBuser\fR (\fB~\fR) or \fBsystem\fR (\fB/opt\fR, sudo). | | \fBpins\fR | map | machine | \fB{}\fR | Binding-pins (\fBcomponent: via\fR) and provider-pins (\fBcapability: component\fR). | | \fBdriver-preference\fR | list | uniform | built-in | Order ties between equally-valid install methods break in; replaces the whole list per layer. | | \fBdisabled-drivers\fR | list | machine | \fB[]\fR | Vias to turn OFF here — their bindings stop matching (a soft \fBsuggests:\fR is skipped, a hard \fBrequires:\fR errors). E.g. \fBdotfiles\fR/\fBglue\fR to manage your own shell config, or \fBsnap\fR. | | \fBauto-tighten\fR | bool | uniform | off | Auto-pick a floor-satisfying install method (versioned \fBrequires:\fR) instead of only advising. | | \fBadopt-installed\fR | bool | uniform | on | Prefer an already-installed method/provider over the default (the detection tier). | | \fBrefresh-before-execute\fR | scalar | uniform | \fBauto\fR | Refresh the OS package index once before staged ops: \fBauto\fR (when the batch has a native install/upgrade), \fBalways\fR, \fBnever\fR. | | \fBinstall-overlay\fR | bool | uniform | on | Open TUI Profiles with the install-state overlay on (\fBO\fR toggles). | | \fBreboot-advice\fR | bool | uniform | on | After an op (and as a TUI chip), advise when a reboot / service restart is needed, via the distro's native check. | | \fBsplash\fR | scalar | uniform | \fBbraille-bar\fR | Startup wait-screen: a splash-provider name, \fBrandom\fR, \fBoff\fR, or unset for the built-in default. | | \fBeffects\fR | scalar | machine | auto | TUI motion: \fBfull\fR (gradient + splash), \fBreduced\fR, \fBnone\fR. Unset auto-picks \fBreduced\fR over SSH. | | \fBorphans-ignore\fR | list | machine | \fB[]\fR | Name-or-glob patterns whose orphans stay quiet in \fBconfigsys orphans\fR. | | \fBdirs\fR | map | mixed | see below | Install-layout dirs: \fBuser\fR/\fBsystem\fR (machine), \fBapp\fR/\fBsdk\fR/\fBsrc\fR (uniform). | | \fBlocations\fR | map | hand-edited | \fB{}\fR | Per-component absolute install-location override (\fBcomponent: path\fR). | | \fBdetect-coexisting\fR | bool | hand-edited | on | Probe each component's OTHER install methods after inspect and surface "also present (unmanaged)". | | \fBinstaller-shell-writes\fR | scalar | hand-edited | \fBblock\fR | \fBblock\fR: snapshot/revert rc files an installer scribbles in, staging the removed block as inactive glue; \fBallow\fR turns the guard off. | | \fBinstaller-shell-writes-allow\fR | list | hand-edited | \fB[]\fR | Components exempt from the shell-writes guard. | | \fBversion-floors\fR | map | hand-edited | \fB{}\fR | Maintainer-authored minimum-version \fBrequires:\fR tightening (\fB{ component: { cap: ">=X" } }\fR). | | \fBfacets\fR | map | hand-edited | \fB{}\fR | Declared detected-environment atoms usable in \fBwhen:\fR (docs/facets.md). | | \fBkeys\fR | map | cosmetic | built-in | TUI keybinding overrides, merged from every layer (\fBconfigsys keys\fR shows the result). | | \fBtheme\fR | map | cosmetic | built-in | TUI palette/roles/gradients, merged from every layer (docs/theming.md). |
.PP
A \fBhand-edited\fR setting is merged like the others (repo < primary < your config, later wins) but is not offered by \fBconfigsys config set\fR / the Config screen — edit the file.
.PP
Notes on a few:
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBdirs:\fR\fR relocates the install-layout directories without a shell env var. Keys: \fBuser\fR (user-scope base, default \fB~\fR), \fBsystem\fR (system-scope base, default \fB/opt\fR), and the category dirs \fBapp\fR/\fBsdk\fR/\fBsrc\fR (defaults \fBapps\fR/\fBsdks\fR/\fBsrc\fR, referenced in routes as \fB$CONFIGSYS_APP_DIR\fR etc.). Precedence is \fBdefault < \fBdirs:\fR < env\fR — the \fBCONFIGSYS_USERSCOPE_DIR\fR / \fBCONFIGSYS_SYSTEMSCOPE_DIR\fR / \fBCONFIGSYS_APP_DIR\fR / \fBCONFIGSYS_SDK_DIR\fR / \fBCONFIGSYS_SRC_DIR\fR env vars still win, so \fBdirs:\fR is the durable setting and the env var the per-invocation override. (Bootstrap paths — where the config/state/repo live — stay env-only; they're needed before the config loads.)
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBlocations:\fR\fR is "find/manage THIS component's install here": an absolute, scope-bypassing path honored by the path-based drivers (source builds, appImage, tarball, font, and the build plugins) over their computed dir. Distinct from \fBdirs:\fR, which relocates a whole category.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBdetect-coexisting:\fR\fR — package managers are enumerated once (batched), so the cost is a handful of "list installed" calls, not one per component. \fBconfigsys versions <name>\fR shows per-method state regardless.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBpins:\fR\fR reroutes without redefining: a binding-pin forces a component's driver, a provider-pin forces which component satisfies a capability. \fBconfigsys pin set|unset|promote\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBsplash:\fR\fR — fancier splashes are shipped by code plugins (\fBSPLASHES\fR export — e.g. \fBconfigsys-splash-ocean\fR; see docs/plugins.md) and must be trusted before they load; \fBCONFIGSYS_NO_SPLASH\fR, \fB--nocolor\fR, \fB--effects none\fR and \fB--verbose\fR also suppress the splash.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBcomponents:\fR\fR \fIamends\fR a route: bindings merge \fBadditively\fR across layers by \fB(via, when)\fR identity — a higher layer adds an install method, overrides a matching binding, retracts one with a \fBdrop:\fR binding, or removes the whole component with \fB{}\fR.
.SH WHERE A MACHINE-SETTING EDIT LANDS
.PP
\fBconfigsys config set …\fR and the TUI \fBConfig\fR screen route each setting by its \fBnature\fR (the table above):
.IP \(bu 2
\fBuniform\fR settings are the same on every machine, so an edit defaults into your \fBprimary plugin\fR (portable — commit + push + re-tag the primary + \fBplugin sync\fR to propagate; it's effective on this machine immediately). \fBpicks:\fR is uniform too — the install set is meant to travel (\fBconfigsys picks to-primary\fR moves this box's local picks there).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmachine\fR settings are a truth about \fIthis\fR box, so an edit defaults to your \fBtop config\fR (\fB~/.config/configsys/configsys.hu\fR), local and unshared. \fBdispositions:\fR (your NEW/seen triage) and \fBuninstall:\fR are machine-local too.
.PP
When no primary is blessed, everything lands local. An edit to a setting you've \fIalready\fR set goes to the layer it lives in (so a lower layer can't silently shadow it). The Config screen shows each setting's current home (\fBlocal\fR / \fBprimary: <name>\fR / \fBbuilt-in default\fR) and its nature, and \fBconfig show\fR prints the same.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBconfig set <key> <value> --local\fR forces this-machine-only (bypasses the uniform default).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBconfig move <key>\fR (TUI: \fB\fBm\fR\fR) carries a setting the other way — \fBlocal ↔ primary\fR — writing its value at the destination and clearing the source. Direction is inferred from where it lives now; a setting still at its built-in default has nothing to move.
.PP
An \fBenv var always wins\fR over both (the per-invocation escape hatch), and is surfaced as the source when set.
.SH PICKS — WHAT A MACHINE INSTALLS
.PP
The install set is the \fBcomponent × machine matrix\fR: \fBpicks:\fR holds one component list per machine, and \fBmachine:\fR says which column \fIthis\fR box is. A machine installs exactly the components picked for it — nothing is inferred from profiles.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
machine: laptop
picks: {
    laptop:  [ btop, neovim, gcc-15 ]
    desktop: [ btop, neovim, steam, blender ]
    build-box: [ ]                    // an empty column is still a machine
}
.fi
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\fBconfigsys picks list|add|rm [--machine M]\fR edits a column (default: this box's); \fBpicks to-primary\fR moves this box's local picks into the primary plugin so they travel.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBconfigsys machine list|show|add|rm|use\fR manages the columns; \fBmachine use <name>\fR sets this box's \fBmachine:\fR (no name clears it). \fB--machine NAME\fR on any command curates/plans another machine's set for the run; install/execute still act on the local machine only.
.IP \(bu 2
The TUI \fBProfiles\fR screen is the same matrix interactively (\fBt\fR/\fBT\fR toggle picks, \fBm\fR/\fBM\fR choose machines); the \fBComponents\fR screen shows this machine's picks with their state.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBdispositions:\fR\fR is a side-store of triage marks (\fBseen\fR / \fBinteresting\fR; unlisted = NEW) so the catalog can be worked through once — \fBconfigsys disp list|get|set\fR. \fB\fBuninstall:\fR\fR is the queue of components staged for removal (TUI \fBx\fR), run at execute.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBconfigsys orphans\fR lists installed software no pick accounts for; \fB--adopt\fR picks it.
.PP
The retired \fBconfigs:\fR and user-authored \fBprofiles:\fR sections now only produce \fBcheck\fR warnings.
.SH PROFILES — READ-ONLY BROWSE LENSES
.PP
A profile is a named list of components — defined in the repo's \fBconfig.hu\fR or by a plugin — used to \fBbrowse\fR the catalog (\fBconfigsys profile list|show\fR, the TUI Profiles screen). Browsing a profile never changes your system; you pick from it. On the command line, \fBprofile:<name>\fR expands to a profile's members (\fBconfigsys install profile:dev\fR).
.PP
For repo/plugin authors, a profile's value is a list of \fBterms\fR, applied left-to-right:
.IP \(bu 2
a bare \fBname\fR adds a component;
.IP \(bu 2
\fB+name\fR splices in another profile's members (recursively);
.IP \(bu 2
\fB~name\fR removes a component (or a whole spliced sub-profile) added so far;
.IP \(bu 2
\fB+self\fR means "the same profile from the next layer down", so a higher layer \fBamends\fR a profile in place instead of replacing it wholesale.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
profiles: {
    base: [ btop, git, curl ]
    dev:  [ +base, neovim, gcc-15, ~curl ]   // base, plus tools, minus curl
    dev:  [ +self, valgrind ]                // (higher plugin layer) amend dev in place
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
Order matters: a \fB~\fR after a \fB+\fR drops what the include brought in; a later add re-adds it.
.PP
\fB\fBall\fR\fR is a built-in synthetic profile — every defined component. You don't declare it; use it as \fBconfigsys install profile:all\fR, or cycle the Components screen's view mode (\fBM\fR) to see the full installed+tracked picture.
.SH COMPONENTS AND BINDINGS
.PP
\fBroutes.hu\fR's \fBcomponents:\fR section defines each component as a capability plus a list of context-selected \fBbindings\fR. A binding names its \fBdriver\fR with \fBvia:\fR, guards itself with an optional \fBwhen:\fR expression, and carries driver-specific details:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
components: {
    btop: {
        install: [
            { via: native  when: "rhel"  requires: epel }   // EL: needs EPEL enabled
            { via: native  repo-component: universe }        // everywhere else
        ]
    }
    chrome:     { install: [ { via: flatpak  hub: flathub  app: com.google.Chrome } ] }
    discord:    { install: [ { via: flatpak  hub: flathub  app: com.discordapp.Discord }
                             { via: native-pkg-file  when: debian  standing: never-auto  url: "…" } ] }
    vulkan-dev: { install: [ { via: parts  parts: [ build-essential, vulkan-sdk ] } ] }
}
.fi
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBvia: native\fR\fR resolves to whatever the OS uses (apt / dnf / pacman / zypper / apk / brew) — one route covers every distro. \fBname:\fR maps the package name per driver.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBvia: parts\fR\fR is a pure aggregator: a component that is just the union of its parts, with no unit of its own.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBrequires:\fR\fR pulls in capabilities (another component, or a driver's prerequisites) first, dependency-ordered — a hard dependency (unmet is an error). \fB\fBsuggests:\fR\fR is soft (pulled in if resolvable here, skipped otherwise). \fB\fBprovides:\fR\fR declares extra capabilities a component satisfies; a versioned capability is \fBprovides: { cap: N }\fR matched by \fBrequires: { cap: ">=N" }\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBstanding:\fR\fR is the one preference knob, on a binding, a driver, or a component: \fBnever-auto\fR keeps a method valid, listed and pinnable but never the auto-default (on a component, never auto-pulled to satisfy a \fBrequires:\fR); an \fBinteger\fR is a preference rank (higher wins). See "Choosing among methods".
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBscope: user|system\fR\fR sets the install scope. Scope-honoring drivers (appImage, flatpak, tarball, source, font, npm, gem, luarocks) default to \fBuser\fR; fixed-scope drivers (apt/dnf/pacman = system, cargo/pipx/dotfiles/glue = user) ignore it.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBdescription:\fR\fR (one line) and \fB\fBattrs:\fR\fR (kind tags — CLI/TUI/GUI/FOSS/…; see docs/component-attrs.md) describe a component for the catalog and its filters.
.PP
Bindings \fBmerge additively\fR across layers. A binding's identity is its \fB(via, when)\fR pair, so a higher layer (a plugin or your config) can \fIadd\fR a new install method, \fIoverride\fR a matching binding, \fIretract\fR an inherited one with a \fBdrop:\fR binding (\fB{ via: X  when: Y  drop }\fR), or clear the whole component with \fB{}\fR — none of which requires redefining the component.
.PP
Among the bindings that are \fBvalid\fR here (their \fBwhen:\fR matches), one is chosen — see "Choosing among methods" below. Run \fBconfigsys where <name>\fR to see a component's bindings, which are valid here, and which one resolves.
.SH THE \FBWHEN:\FR EXPRESSION
.PP
\fBwhen:\fR is a boolean expression that selects a binding by machine context. Its atoms are:
.IP \(bu 2
an \fBOS name\fR — bare (\fBubuntu\fR, \fBredhat\fR) matches that block and everything that inherits from it; versioned (\fBubuntu < 23.04\fR) matches a version range on a scale;
.IP \(bu 2
a \fBCPU\fR atom (\fBcpu: aarch64\fR);
.IP \(bu 2
a declared \fBfacet\fR atom — any other detected fact about the machine (a GPU vendor, a tool's version), declared in a \fBfacets:\fR section; see docs/facets.md.
.PP
Combine atoms with \fBand\fR, \fBor\fR, and a guarded \fBnot\fR. OS blocks form a lineage via \fBusing:\fR (\fBpop_os! -> ubuntu -> debian -> linux\fR), detected from \fB/etc/os-release\fR (\fBID=pop\fR -> \fBpop_os!\fR), so a route written on \fBdebian\fR applies to the whole family. \fBwhen:\fR expresses \fBvalidity only\fR — whether a method works here — never which method to prefer. Two bindings of the \fBsame driver\fR whose match-sets overlap must be comparable (one more specific than the other) or configsys reports a load-time ambiguity; overlapping bindings of \fIdifferent\fR drivers are legal alternatives, decided by preference (see below).
.SH CHOOSING AMONG METHODS
.PP
A component can have several valid install methods in one context (e.g. \fBnative\fR and \fBflatpak\fR both work on Ubuntu). configsys picks one default deterministically, in this order:
.PP
0. bindings marked \fB\fBstanding: never-auto\fR\fR are set aside (unless every valid method is); 1. \fBmost specific\fR among comparable valid bindings (a narrower \fBwhen:\fR beats a broader one); 2. a per-binding \fB\fBstanding:\fR\fR integer rank — a narrow, per-package author signal, so it outranks the blanket order below; 3. \fB\fBdriver-preference\fR\fR — the global tiebreak list (a machine setting, overridable per OS block).
.PP
If that still ties, it is an error that names the preference channel — never a prompt to narrow a validity \fBwhen:\fR. Above the default sit two overrides: with \fBadopt-installed\fR on (the default), a method that is \fBalready installed\fR is adopted over the computed default; and a \fBpin\fR beats everything (\fBconfigsys pin set <name> <driver>\fR writes a binding-pin, or edit \fBpins:\fR directly). Precedence: \fBpin > installed > standing default\fR. \fBconfigsys where <name>\fR shows all candidates and which rule decided.
.PP
A derivative distro that does not rebrand \fB/etc/os-release\fR (Proxmox VE reports \fBID=debian\fR) can still be detected by a \fBmarker\fR: an os block declares \fBdetect: { id: <base>  marker: <path> }\fR (marker may be a list). When the detected block is that base — or a descendant — and every marker exists on disk, configsys routes to the more-specific block. This is the data-driven form of the built-in Fedora-Atomic ostree-marker detection, so a plugin can add a detectable OS with no code (the \fBconfigsys-proxmox\fR plugin does exactly this).
.SH VERSIONS — DISCOVERED, NOT HARDCODED
.PP
Download-based bindings declare \fIhow\fR to find the latest version rather than pinning one:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
neovim: {
    install: [ { via: appImage  name: Neovim  scope: user
                 version: { github: neovim/neovim  asset: "nvim-linux-$ARCH.appimage" }
                 url: "https://github.com/neovim/neovim/releases/download/$VERSION/nvim-linux-$ARCH.appimage" } ]
}
.fi
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fB{ github: owner/repo }\fR\fR — the latest release tag (pre-release tags skipped); optional \fBstrip-v\fR; optional \fBasset: <glob>\fR also resolves the exact download URL from the release assets.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fB{ url: "..."  regex: "..." }\fR\fR — fetch a page and extract the version.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fB{ static: "..." }\fR\fR — a deliberate pin.
.PP
\fB$VERSION\fR and \fB$ARCH\fR are filled into the URL at install time. When a \fBgithub\fR asset name is literal (no glob), configsys can fall back to the API-free \fBreleases/latest/download/<asset>\fR URL, so installs keep working when the GitHub API is unreachable. Discovered versions are cached (\fB~/.config/configsys/versions.hu\fR, 24h TTL); \fBconfigsys refresh\fR re-queries (and also refreshes the native package index); \fBconfigsys versions <name>\fR shows what every method would install; and \fB--pretend\fR never touches the network (cache-only). Set \fBCONFIGSYS_GITHUB_TOKEN\fR (or \fBGITHUB_TOKEN\fR) to lift GitHub's unauthenticated rate limit.
.SH DOTFILES
.PP
A \fBvia: dotfiles\fR component manages an application's \fBown config\fR. It maps link specs \fB{ src, dst }\fR: \fBdst\fR is where the config belongs (env-var and \fB~\fR expanded), and \fBsrc\fR is resolved through a content \fBsearch-path\fR — the first of these that has it wins:
.IP \(bu 2
\fB~/.config/configsys/dotfiles/<component>.cfs/<src>\fR — your machine-local store
.IP \(bu 2
\fB<primary plugin>/dotfiles/<component>.cfs/<src>\fR — your portable, git-tracked config
.IP \(bu 2
\fB<defining layer>/dotfiles/<component>.cfs/<src>\fR — a template, only if some layer ships one
.PP
The \fB\fB<component>.cfs/\fR marker dir\fR is what makes a config \fImanaged\fR: it holds the content plus a \fBmanifest.hu\fR recording the src→dst layout and any \fBexclude:\fR globs (secret-shaped files are suggested into it on first capture). A managed config with no files yet is still managed — the marker, not the content, is the state.
.PP
configsys ships \fBno personal config templates\fR: a component may declare \fBsrc\fR/\fBdst\fR with no content anywhere, in which case it is simply not linked (a no-op) until you supply content. Install symlinks \fBdst -> src\fR so edits flow back to git; links never point into the repo.
.PP
Because your existing config is precious, install \fBrefuses\fR to symlink over a real on-system file that resolves only to an un-adopted template, printing how to adopt it (\fBconfigsys dotfiles capture\fR) — or \fBinstall --force\fR to back it up to \fB<name>.pre-configsys\fR and replace. Adopted content (in your store) links freely; an \fBabsorb-into\fR spec relocates a pre-existing file into a loader dir instead of backing it up (e.g. a stray \fB~/.bash_aliases\fR). \fBuninstall\fR removes the symlink and restores any backup.
.PP
\fBconfigsys dotfiles status\fR reports each dotfile's state — \fBlinked\fR / \fBadopted\fR / \fBunmanaged\fR / \fBtemplate\fR / \fBempty\fR — and where its managed content lives; \fBconfigsys dotfiles capture\fR copies your existing on-system dotfiles into your store (read-only on the system side). The TUI \fBDotfiles\fR screen does the same per row (\fBm\fR/\fBu\fR manage/unmanage, \fBM\fR/\fBU\fR all, \fBs\fR/\fBS\fR move between the local and primary stores). A package that ships config \fBsuggests:\fR its \fB<name>-dotfiles\fR component (soft, so it attaches only where that config exists).
.SH GLUE
.PP
A \fBvia: glue\fR component is the \fBshell integration\fR a tool needs to be usable — PATH, an alias (\fBfdfind\fR→\fBfd\fR, \fBbatcat\fR→\fBbat\fR), an init hook (\fBzoxide init\fR), completions — as a small snippet per shell:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
zoxide-glue: { install: [ { via: glue  requires: shell-glue  glue: zoxide } ] }
.fi
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
Glue is \fBshipped, never captured\fR: a snippet is authored at \fBglue/shell/<shell>/<name>.<ext>\fR in the repo or a plugin, materialized into the machine-local glue store (\fB~/.config/configsys/glue/<shell>/conf.d/\fR), and linked into the uniform \fB~/.config/<shell>/conf.d/\fR for each \fBinstalled\fR shell that has a variant. elvish and nushell can't source a directory, so their snippets are inlined into the rc file instead.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBshell-glue\fR\fR is the one loader component: it wires every installed shell to source its \fBconf.d\fR (bash via \fB~/.bash_aliases\fR, zsh via an rc marker block, fish natively). Every snippet \fBrequires:\fR it.
.IP \(bu 2
A tool \fBsuggests:\fR its \fB<name>-glue\fR companion, so the snippet attaches only where the tool is picked. Glue is "active" or not — it has no version — and lives on its own TUI \fBGlue\fR screen (\fBa\fR/\fBA\fR activate, \fBx\fR deactivate), grouped per shell. \fBCONFIGSYS_GLUE_SHELLS\fR overrides which shells count as installed.
.IP \(bu 2
Opt out per machine with \fBdisabled-drivers: [ glue ]\fR (and/or \fBdotfiles\fR) to manage your own shell config; the guard below keeps installers from scribbling in it.
.PP
\fBInstaller shell writes.\fR With \fBinstaller-shell-writes: block\fR (the default), an installer that appends to \fB~/.bashrc\fR / \fB~/.zshrc\fR / \fB~/.profile\fR (sdkman, nvm, rustup, conda, …) has the file reverted to its prior bytes and the removed block \fBstaged\fR as an inactive glue candidate: \fBconfigsys dotfiles staged\fR lists them, \fBactivate\fR links one into \fBconf.d\fR, \fBdiscard\fR drops it. \fBinstaller-shell-writes-allow\fR exempts named components.
.SH COMPONENT-NAMES
.PP
A higher layer (typically a plugin) can patch the package name a component maps to under a given driver — or drop it where that driver has no package — without redefining the whole component:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
component-names: {
    xbps: { docker-engine: docker  r: R  nmap: {} }   // rename; {} = no package here (drop)
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
Keyed by driver and overlaid across the layer stack (later wins): a string replaces the resolved package name; \fB{}\fR (or null) means "not available via this driver," so the component isn't offered there. See the routing model (docs/routing-model.md §10a) for the full rules.
.SH DRIVERS
.PP
Each \fBvia:\fR value names a driver: the OS package managers (\fBapt\fR, \fBdnf\fR, \fBpacman\fR, \fBzypper\fR, \fBapk\fR, \fBaur\fR, \fBbrew\fR, \fBrpm-ostree\fR), the distribution drivers \fBflatpak\fR, \fBsnap\fR, \fBappImage\fR, \fBtarball\fR (also bare-binary and \fB.zip\fR archives), \fBnative-pkg-file\fR (an upstream release's \fB.deb\fR/\fB.rpm\fR installed with the OS package tool — distinct from repo \fBnative\fR, since it doesn't ride \fBapt upgrade\fR), \fBsource\fR (build from a git checkout or source archive with declared \fBbuild:\fR commands), \fBfont\fR, \fBscript\fR (declared install/version/uninstall commands); the config drivers \fBdotfiles\fR and \fBglue\fR; the language toolchains and their module/version installers (\fBcargo\fR, \fBpip\fR, \fBpipx\fR, \fBnpm\fR, \fBgem\fR, \fBgo-install\fR, \fBopam\fR, \fBluarocks\fR, \fBcabal\fR, \fBpyenv\fR, \fBsdkman\fR, \fBgcc\fR, \fBclang\fR, \fBgcc-toolset\fR); and the post-install primitives \fBservice\fR (systemd) and \fBgroup\fR (usermod). Two \fBvia:\fR values are special: \fBnative\fR (resolves to the OS's package manager) and \fBparts\fR (a pure aggregator). Plugins may add drivers.
.SH LOCAL PLUGINS
.PP
To let a source repo or an app install contribute components (or os/driver blocks) to \fIthis\fR machine only, declare it as a \fBlocal plugin\fR in your top config rather than your portable primary:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
configsys plugin add github:owner/their-configsys-plugin --local
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fB--local\fR writes the entry to \fB~/.config/configsys/configsys.hu\fR's \fBplugins:\fR list, so it applies here and doesn't travel to your other machines the way a \fBprimary\fR plugin does. It loads as a \fBplugin\fR-role layer: it may add \fBcomponents:\fR/\fBprofiles:\fR/\fBos:\fR/\fBdrivers:\fR, but not machine settings (those stay \fBprimary\fR/top-config only), and code plugins still require an explicit \fBconfigsys plugin trust\fR. Without \fB--local\fR, \fBplugin add\fR rides your primary plugin when one is set. See docs/plugins.md for the plugin model.
.SH SEE ALSO
.PP
\fBconfigsys(1)\fR for the command-line interface; the README for a narrative overview.
